// Per-construct DSL layout: every <kind>s.memql file in a domain holds every
// construct of that kind in the domain. This domain is the PROVING SUITE's
// published record: what each benchmark run measured, and -- just as
// deliberately -- what it did not.
//
// Design record: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-06-proving-suite-design.md.
// The Go side is component/proving/ and cmd/memql-bench.

/// One invocation of the proving suite. Written by `memql-bench --do=run`,
/// once per run, before any sample.
///
/// CLUSTER-OWNER TIER, and it has no owner field at all. A benchmark is a fact
/// about the DEPLOYMENT rather than about a person: there is nobody whose row
/// this is, so the composite `owner=..., clusterOwner` form other operator
/// surfaces use would have an owner key that nothing could ever fill.
///
/// EVERY MUTATION BOUND TO THIS CONCEPT IS @serverOnly, and that is the
/// load-bearing part. These rows are what the README's published claims rest
/// on (the claims gate in scorecard/claims.go checks a marked claim against
/// the committed scorecard), so a client-reachable write here would be a
/// primitive for forging the numbers the product is sold on. The writer stamps
/// internal origin and takes an entry in call_origin_conformance_test.go's
/// allowlist saying exactly that.
@displayCard(primary="tier", secondary="verdict", tertiary="commit", status="verdict")
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner)
concept run {
  tier               enum("ci", "live")!  @description("Which lane produced this run. `ci` replays from recorded responses and reaches no provider; `live` runs against real providers. The two measure different things about the same metric names, which is why a figure from one is never compared against a figure from the other -- component/proving/figure.Compare returns `undecidable` across a tier boundary rather than pretending.")
  commit             string!  @description("Short SHA the suite ran at. Required on every figure: a number nobody can reproduce is a number nobody should act on.")
  corpusFingerprint  string!  @description("Digest over the scenario corpus's CONTENT, from component/proving/scenario. Two runs with different fingerprints measured different things, and joining them into a trend is the mistake this field exists to make visible. It moves when an asserted value changes and stays put when the JSON is merely reformatted.")
  scenarioCount      int!  @description("How many scenarios ran. A run whose count fell without a corpus change is a run that skipped something.")
  verdict            enum("pass", "blocked", "error")!  @description("`pass` means no blocking failure; `blocked` means a structural property regressed, a verifier failed, or a negative control read zero; `error` means the suite could not run. Cost and speed movements never produce `blocked` -- design P2, because a threshold that reds the lane for runner noise gets widened until it means nothing, taking the structural half with it.")
  modelIds           []string  @description("The models involved, sorted. On a live run these are the providers called. On a CI run they are what the cassettes were RECORDED against -- `synthetic` where the tape holds placeholder responses, which is honest about structure and silent about anything model-dependent.")
  costUsd            float  @description("What this run cost. Absent rather than zero on a CI run, which reaches no provider: an absent figure and a zero are different answers, about money as much as anything else.")
  runner             string  @description("The machine class the run happened on, so a wall-clock figure from a shared CI runner is not silently compared against one from a workstation.")
  blockingFailures   []string  @description("The blocking failures, in the suite's own words. Empty on a pass. A negative control that read zero appears here and is the most serious entry it can carry: it means the counter behind a metric never goes up on any path, so every green figure that metric produced means nothing.")
  startedAt          datetime!  @description("When the run began.")
  finishedAt         datetime  @description("When it ended. Empty on a run that did not finish.")
}

/// One published figure: a metric, on one arm, for one scenario.
///
/// A SAMPLE CARRIES EITHER A STATISTIC OR AN ABSENT REASON, NEVER BOTH AND
/// NEVER NEITHER. That invariant is enforced in Go by component/proving/figure
/// before a row is composed, and it is why `absentReason` is a first-class
/// field rather than an empty median: a run whose provider-call count could
/// not be measured and a run that genuinely made zero provider calls are
/// different answers, and a schema that could only say "0" would collapse
/// them.
///
/// There is deliberately NO `mean` field, and no single-number form. The epic's
/// honesty rule is medians and spread, never a best case, and a schema that
/// offered a bare number is a schema somebody would eventually publish from.
@displayCard(primary="metric", secondary="arm", tertiary="scenarioId", status="arm")
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner)
concept sample {
  benchRunId    string!  @description("v1:bench:run.id this sample belongs to.")
  family        enum("amortizedCost", "reliability", "recovery", "durability", "learningCurve", "speed", "governance")!  @description("Which of the six families this belongs to, or `governance` -- which is carried by the same rows and is deliberately NOT a family in the epic's sense: it is pass-or-fail and has no statistics, so there is no governance score to be tempted by.")
  scenarioId    string!  @description("The corpus scenario that produced it, which is also the filename under test/proving/scenarios/.")
  arm           enum("platform", "baseline")!  @description("What was under test. `platform` is the work spine with its machinery on; `baseline` is the same model, the same tools and the same scenario in a plain bounded tool loop, with catalog, journal, replay, classifier and healing switched off. There are exactly two and there will not be a third: the epic's comparison is a model by itself against the model with MemQL, and a third arm turns a comparison into a survey.")
  metric        string!  @description("The metric id, registered in component/proving/figure. Registration is what supplies its unit and its DIRECTION -- whether lower or higher is better -- so an unregistered metric cannot be compared at all rather than being compared by a guess.")
  unit          enum("calls", "steps", "tokens", "usd", "ms", "ratio", "percent", "count")!  @description("What the number counts. Two figures in different units are never compared.")
  n             int  @description("Sample size. Absent when the figure is unmeasured.")
  median        float  @description("The median. Absent when the figure is unmeasured -- and note that ABSENT is not 0.0: a reader must consult absentReason before treating this as a number.")
  p10           float  @description("Tenth percentile. Quoted for N of five or more; below that the scorecard quotes the range and the median absolute deviation instead, because a p10 over three samples is an order statistic wearing a percentile's name.")
  p90           float  @description("Ninetieth percentile.")
  minimum       float  @description("Smallest observation. Named `minimum` rather than `min` because `min` reads as an intrinsic.")
  maximum       float  @description("Largest observation.")
  mad           float  @description("Median absolute deviation -- the spread figure quoted for a small N.")
  absentReason  enum("", "notMeasurableOnReplay", "seamNotBuilt", "tierNotRun", "noProvider", "belowFloor", "ceilingReached")  @default("")  @description("Why there is no number. A CLOSED set: a free-text reason drifts into 'n/a' within two releases and stops meaning anything, and the whole value of an absent figure is that it says something specific. `seamNotBuilt` is the one that matters most today -- it names code that does not exist yet, and it is what stops the suite reporting 'zero provider calls' because nothing in the path calls a provider, which is a lie that reads exactly like the headline result.")
  detail        string  @description("Qualifies absentReason. Required for `seamNotBuilt`, where it NAMES the missing code -- 'the code is not built' without naming the code is the sort of absence that never becomes a work item.")
  tier          enum("ci", "live")!  @description("Copied from the run, so a sample is self-describing when read on its own.")
  commit        string!  @description("Copied from the run.")
  measuredOn    string!  @description("The date, YYYY-MM-DD. A date rather than a timestamp: the scorecard is a daily artifact and a timestamp invites reading a precision into it that the refresh cadence does not support.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="benchRunId", target=run, direction="outgoing")
}
